"dblp: Machine Learning in Network Intrusion Detection: A Cross-Dataset Generalization Study. (https://dblp.dagstuhl.de/rec/journals/access/CantoneMB24.html)\nciteturn19search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Machine Learning in Network Intrusion Detection: A Cross-Dataset Generalization Study.\n\n# \"Machine Learning in Network Intrusion Detection: A Cross-Dataset ...\"\n\n  * help us\n\n    * How can I correct errors in dblp?\n    * contact dblp\n\nMarco Cantone, Claudio Marrocco, Alessandro Bria (2024)\n\n\n## Details and statistics\n\n  * show external API response\n\n    * JSON @ openalex.org\n\nsee also:\n\n    * API doc @ openalex.org\n\nDOI: 10.1109/ACCESS.2024.3472907\n\n\nmetadata version: 2024-11-06\n\n  * Image: Journal Articles\n\n    * view\n\n\ndblp key:\n\n      * journals/access/CantoneMB24\n\n    * ask others\n\n      * Google\n      * Google Scholar\n      * Semantic Scholar\n      * Internet Archive Scholar\n      * CiteSeerX\n      * PubPeer\n\n    * share record\n\n      * Bluesky\n      * Reddit\n      * BibSonomy\n      * LinkedIn\n\npersistent URL:\n\n      * https://dblp.org/rec/journals/access/CantoneMB24\n\nMarco CantoneImage: 0000-0002-6225-6680, Claudio MarroccoImage: 0000-0003-0840-7350, Alessandro BriaImage: 0000-0002-2895-6544:\nMachine Learning in Network Intrusion Detection: A Cross-Dataset Generalization Study. IEEE Access 12: 144489-144508 (2024)\n\n\nlast updated on 2024-11-06 22:17 CET by the dblp team\n\n all metadata released as open data under CC0 1.0 license--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMachine Learning in Network Intrusion Detection: A Cross-Dataset Generalization Study | CoLab (https://colab.ws/articles/10.1109%2Faccess.2024.3472907)\nciteturn19search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.0 years ago; Crawled: yesterday; Network Intrusion Detection Systems (NIDS) are a fundamental tool in cybersecurity.Their ability to generalize across diverse networks is a critical factor in their effectiveness and a prerequisite for real-world applications.In this study, we conduct a comprehensive analysis on the generalization of machine-learning-based NIDS through an extensive experimentation in a cross-dataset framework.We employ four machine learning classifiers and utilize four datasets acquired from different networks: CIC-IDS-2017, CSE-CIC-IDS2018, LycoS-IDS2017, and LycoS-Unicas-IDS2018.\n\nOpen Access\n\nImage: Open access\n\nIEEE Access\n\n,  том 12 ,  страницы 144489-144508\n\n# Machine Learning in Network Intrusion Detection: A Cross-Dataset Generalization Study\n\nТип публикации: Journal Article\n\nДата публикации: 2024-10-03\n\nКраткое описание\n\nNetwork Intrusion Detection Systems (NIDS) are a fundamental tool in cybersecurity.Their ability to generalize across diverse networks is a critical factor in their effectiveness and a prerequisite for real-world applications.In this study, we conduct a comprehensive analysis on the generalization of machine-learning-based NIDS through an extensive experimentation in a cross-dataset framework.We employ four machine learning classifiers and utilize four datasets acquired from different networks: CIC-IDS-2017, CSE-CIC-IDS2018, LycoS-IDS2017, and LycoS-Unicas-IDS2018. Notably, the last dataset is a novel contribution, where we apply corrections based on LycoS-IDS2017 to the well-known CSE-CIC-IDS2018 dataset.The results show nearly perfect classification performance when the models are trained and tested on the same dataset.However, when training and testing the models in a cross-dataset fashion, the classification accuracy is largely commensurate with random chance except for a few combinations of attacks and datasets.We employ data visualization techniques in order to provide valuable insights on the patterns in the data.Our analysis unveils the presence of anomalies in the data that directly hinder the classifiers capability to generalize the learned knowledge to new scenarios.This study enhances our comprehension of the generalization capabilities of machine-learning-based NIDS, highlighting the significance of acknowledging data heterogeneity.\n\nCantone M. et al. Machine Learning in Network Intrusion Detection: A Cross-Dataset Generalization Study // IEEE Access. 2024. Vol. 12. pp. 144489-144508.\n\nГОСТ со всеми авторами (до 50) Скопировать\n\nCantone M., MARROCCO C., Bria A. Machine Learning in Network Intrusion Detection: A Cross-Dataset Generalization Study // IEEE Access. 2024. Vol. 12. pp. 144489-144508.\n\nTY - JOUR\n\nDO - 10.1109/access.2024.3472907\n\nUR - https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/10704637/\n\nTI - Machine Learning in Network Intrusion Detection: A Cross-Dataset Generalization Study\n\nT2 - IEEE Access\n\nAU - Cantone, Marco\n\nAU - MARROCCO, C\n\nAU - Bria, Alessandro\n\nPY - 2024\n\nDA - 2024/10/03\n\nPB - Institute of Electrical and Electronics Engineers (IEEE)\n\nSP - 144489-144508\n\nVL - 12\n\nSN - 2169-3536\n\n@article{2024_Cantone,\n\nauthor = {Marco Cantone and C MARROCCO and Alessandro Bria},\n\ntitle = {Machine Learning in Network Intrusion Detection: A Cross-Dataset Generalization Study},\n\njournal = {IEEE Access},\n\nyear = {2024},\n\nvolume = {12},\n\npublisher = {Institute of Electrical and Electronics Engineers (IEEE)},\n\nmonth = {oct},\n\nurl = {https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/10704637/},\n\npages = {144489--144508},\n\ndoi = {10.1109/access.2024.3472907}--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMachine Learning in Network Intrusion Detection: A Cross-Dataset Generalization Study (https://iris.unicas.it/retrieve/a40146bd-61d7-4a77-98c7-cf72f480c839/2024%20-%20Machine%20Learning%20in%20Network%20Intrusion%20Detection%20-%20A%20Cross-Dataset%20Generalization%20Study.pdf)\nciteturn19search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.0 years ago; Crawled: 2.0 years ago; Cantone et al.: Machine Learning in Network Intrusion Detection: A Cross-Dataset Generalization Study ... The research by Gu and Lu [22] introduced a novel intrusion detection framework that integrates SVM with naïve Bayes feature embedding.\n\nIn this work, we extensively analyze the generalization capabilities of ML-based-NIDS including a variety of training strategies, data sources and evaluation metrics. Generalization refers to the ability of a model to perform well on data it has never seen during training. For this investigation, we design two types of experiments: a within-dataset scenario in which we split a dataset in train and test sets, and a cross-dataset scenario where train and test sets are taken from different datasets. The datasets we utilize include CIC-IDS2017, CSE-CIC-IDS2018 [38], LycoS-IDS2017 [39], and LycoS-Unicas-IDS2018 generated for this research. These datasets are available both as raw network traffic and features-processed format computed on bidirectional flows. We utilize the available extracted features for training the models, which is the prevailing approach adopted in the literature [14], [15], [20], [21], [22], [24], [28], [29], [40], [41], [42]. The selection of CIC-IDS2017 and CSE-CIC-IDS2018 was based on the presence of similar attack classes, making them suitable for cross-dataset evaluation. We also employed LycoS-IDS2017 to investigate generalization as it offers a distinct feature set resulting from the refinement of specific features from CIC-IDS2017.\n\nOur main contributions are:\n1) Cross-dataset analysis: we evaluated ML-based NIDS generalization capabilities when trained and tested across 4 different datasets. The analysis was carried out encompassing a variety of ML models, feature sets, and training configurations including the discrimination between benign and malicious instances and single-attack analysis.\n2) New dataset: we introduced LycoS-Unicas-IDS2018, an enhanced CSE-CIC-IDS2018 dataset with refined feature extraction techniques, specifically addressing the concerns highlighted by Rosay et al. [39].\n\n144490  